-- French. The planner's second language.
--
-- Dan, 21 August 2026: "Please work on French next?"
--
-- MUCH CHEAPER THAN GERMAN, and that was the point of the ordering.
-- German went first because it is the length stress test and because the
-- FIRST added language is the one that pays for all the structural work:
-- eighteen standing invariants restated, a coverage counter that
-- disagreed with an invariant about benchmark labels, and four tests
-- using the untranslated language as their fixture. None of that
-- recurred here. French is data.
--
-- French runs 17% longer than English, a shade more than German's 15%,
-- and the layout that survived German survives this.
--
-- THE VOCABULARY IS THE SITE'S OWN, read out of i18n/fr.json rather than
-- invented: obligation for mandate, échéance for deadline, pays for
-- jurisdiction, facturation électronique, conformité, télédéclaration.
-- A reader crosses from the tracker to the planner in one click.
--
-- ZERO IS SINGULAR IN FRENCH. Intl.PluralRules says so, the page has
-- selected on CLDR categories since migration 573, and
-- tests/roi-regression.mjs asserts it -- so this needs no extra rows,
-- only the knowledge that it is already handled.
--
-- QUOTED SOURCE MATERIAL STAYS IN ENGLISH, as in German: the citations
-- quote HMRC, the ATO, APQC, Ardent and the OECD verbatim inside
-- &ldquo;&rdquo;, and the prose around them is what is translated.
--
-- BENCHMARK LABELS ARE THE EMPTY STRING. getRoiBenchmarks reads only
-- hint and citation; a standing invariant forbids a non-English label,
-- and the column is NOT NULL.
--
-- GENERATED by gen598.mjs; the English was never retyped.


-- ---- 433 page strings ------------------------------------------------
